 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:15:12.297105Z",
 "text": "Under the Ontario *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*, this clause is void and cannot be enforced. \n\nSubsection 106(6) of the Act requires a landlord to pay interest to the tenant annually on the amount of the rent deposit, calculated at a rate equal to the provincial rent increase guideline in effect when the payment becomes due. Because subsection 4(1) voids any lease term that conflicts with the Act, a landlord cannot waive or contract out of the obligation to pay annual interest on a last month's rent deposit. (In practice, if the rent increases by the guideline amount, section 106(3) allows the landlord to require the tenant to top up the deposit, which usually offsets the interest owed—but the statutory obligation to credit interest remains mandatory.)\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 106",
